I would really like to understand why this is happening? 

A: The issue you're encountering might be related to the way NUnit compiles its tests into the assembly. When you include a NUnit test in your class, it doesn't actually get included in the method you're testing (like ArrayListTest.ArraylistAdd method). 

The "PUT_AddContract" method that you posted is likely not available in the assembly because it's not present at the compile-time. Even if it was included in an actual test class, the framework might not support that attribute, and the method might not be found and executed correctly.

There could also be a conflict with other NUnit attributes that you're using. NUnit framework provides various attributes like TestFixture, Test etc. which you can use with your test methods.

In order to solve this issue:


*

*Make sure your test method follows the syntax and convention (public void TestMethod_[YourScenario]_[ExpectedBehaviour]_[ActualBehaviour]..) as NUnit uses these conventions to figure out what you are trying to test. 

*Look into why the NUnit framework is not detecting your test case. This might help narrow down whether it’s something to do with NUnit not being configured correctly or the test method not having the correct attribute.

*Ensure that all the NUnit classes and assemblies referenced from your unit tests are built before running the tests.  NUnit might not handle changes in the referenced assemblies gracefully if they are not built before or if the binaries have not been refreshed.

*If your unit test project is set to Build Action - Content, then that could cause tests not being pick up. Change it to "Copy to Output Directory" or "Build action" to "Build".

*Ensure the correct version of NUnit library is being referenced in your unit test project.


Remember that each unit testing framework has its own rules about what constitutes a valid test method name. Different frameworks have different ways of distinguishing between setup, cleanup, and individual test cases, for example. It might be easier to consider using a framework that is more straightforward and has more comprehensive documentation or stick to a framework that fits your project needs best. This will help in managing your tests in a more organized and maintainable way.
Remember to always be diligent on these test naming conventions as they will make your test runs more efficient and understandable. Good practices like "TestMethodName_ExpectedBehaviour_ActualBehavior" helps in identifying the test easily. 

