## Add, Remove Contracts


#### ArrayList Add Contract

```csharp
// PUT_AddContract
[Test, PexMethod, ExpectedException(typeof(PexAssertFailedException))]
public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeUnderTest]int x) 
{
    int index = arrList.Add(x);
    // assert a condition that is not expected to hold
    Assert.IsTrue(index - arrList.IndexOf(x) >= 0); 
    // if the assertion fails, Pex will generate a counter example that can be
    // used to test the faulty implementation
}
```

#### ArrayList Remove Contract
```csharp
// PUT_RemoveContract
[Test, PexMethod, ExpectedException(typeof(PexAssertFailedException))]
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, [PexAssumeUnderTest]int x) 
{
    if (!arrList.Contains(x))
        return;
    arrList.Remove(x);
    // assert a condition that is not expected to hold
    Assert.IsTrue(arrList.LastIndexOf(x) >= 0);
    // if the assertion fails, Pex will generate a counter example
}
```
## PexAssertFailedException

When Pex finds an input that makes a failed assert, it generates a PexAssertFailedException. This exception contains information about the failed check and the specific input that made the test fail. With NUnit, you can handle this exception and fail the test, or you can ignore it and continue testing the next batch of inputs. An example of handling this exception in NUnit would be: 

```csharp
[Test, PexMethod]
public void YourTestMethod()
{
    try
    {
        YourArrayListTestMethod();
    }
    catch (PexAssertFailedException ex)
    {

        var failureInfo = (PexAssertFailedException)ex.InnerException.InnerException;

        Console.WriteLine("Failed assertion: " + failureInfo.Message);
        // Add your own test failure handling here
    }
}
``` 


## Observing the Results

Pex provides the ability to observe the results of a method or code block. It provides a range of tools to perform this. Observing allows you to view the values of variables in conjunction with Pex operations. 

Here's an example of an observation:

```csharp
// Add a new int value to the array list,
// observe the returned value, Count and the Index of the new value
int ret = arrList.Add(x);

PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old_ret", ret);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old_arrListCount", arrList.Count);
PexObserve.ValueForViewing("old_arrListIndexOfX", arrList.IndexOf(x));
```

## Contract Testing

A Contract Test involves validating the contracts between two parties. For an ArrayList implementation, this can include confirming the postconditions for each method (for example, that the Count property of an ArrayList is incremented after an Add method is called).

While NUnit and Pex can automate some aspects of this process, it is a complex manual process that involves creating test classes and methods, and ensuring the proper test methods are called. Therefore, creating a thorough contract test suite for an ArrayList implementation that covers all of the methods can be a non-trivial task. Contract testing should be performed by at least two developers per contract (at the very least, the client and the implementation producer) to ensure correct and consistent testing.

## Example Tests

For example, you could have a test like the following:

```csharp
[Test, PexMethod]
public void AddTest([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList list, int val)
{
    int oldCount = list.Count;
    int ret = list.Add(val);
    int newCount = list.Count;
 
    // Add the values to be observed
    PexObserve.Value("old_count", oldCount);
    PexObserve.Value("new_count", newCount);
    PexObserve.Value("ret", ret);   

    Assert.AreEqual(oldCount + 1, newCount);
    Assert.AreEqual(ret, oldCount);
}
```

